These challenges focus on reverse engineering Java code! The first challenge was introductory and only required copy/pasting from the file.
The second challenge still had the literal characters still visible, so it was still trivial, if a little bothersome to extract the characters.
I extracted the code with the characters in a separate file and then ran 4 commands to make it somewhat easier on myself:

1. grep -oP "the code with escape characters to account for parentheticals and whitespace" to isolate the characters.
2. sed -E "the output from the previous command" to strip out everything but the order index and the character.
3. sort -n -k1 to sort by the order index and guarantee it using numeric sort, instead of spaces.
4. awk '{printf "%s", $2} END{print ""}' to print all of the characters in the second column (the actual password characters) with no spaces.

The next challenge has the password visible as an anagram. The source code showed how the characters of a correct password were to be manipulated.
It was simple enough to solve by hand, although a script could do the same. Challenge 4 similarly had ASCII characters plainly visible, so I used
an online ASCII converter to get the decimal, hex, and octal values converted.

Challenge 5 has ASCII characters represented in hex and converted into base64. I reconverted and then got the plaintext from the hexcodes.

For challenge 6, I originally converted to ASCII first, but later noticed that the character was being XORed by a constant, so I built a short
script to XOR every character to get the flag.

Challenge 7 required several steps; Converting a decimal value to binary, converting that binary value to hex, and then converting that hex value to
ASCII. I was momentarily tripped up by the fact that the leading 0s were stripped from the online converter I used, but no problem.
